      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{2}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=1$ and $k=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,-4).$",
